Name :
================
Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)

Why we did this
================
After securing DHCP using DHCP Snooping, attackers discovered another Layer 2 weakness called ARP Spoofing.

ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) does not verify whether an ARP Reply is legitimate.

An attacker can send fake ARP messages such as:

"I am the Default Gateway"

or

"I own the IP Address 192.168.1.1"

As a result:
- Users send traffic to the attacker.
- Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks become possible.
- Sensitive data can be captured.
- Network communications can be manipulated.

To prevent this, Cisco introduced Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI).

DAI validates every ARP packet before forwarding it.

What will happen
================
1. Switch receives an ARP packet.
2. DAI checks the ARP packet against the DHCP Snooping Binding Table.
3. The IP-MAC mapping is verified.
4. If the ARP packet is valid:
   - Packet is forwarded.
5. If the ARP packet is invalid:
   - Packet is dropped.
   - Log entry is generated.
6. ARP Spoofing attacks are blocked.

Example

Without DAI
-----------

Gateway = 192.168.1.1

Attacker sends:

192.168.1.1 = AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA

Users update their ARP table.

Result:
--------
Traffic redirected to attacker.


With DAI
--------

Attacker sends fake ARP packet.

DAI checks DHCP Snooping Database.

Database Entry:

192.168.1.1 = 00:11:22:33:44:55

Received ARP:

192.168.1.1 = AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA

Mismatch Detected

Result:
--------
ARP Packet Dropped

Attack Prevented

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Prevents ARP Spoofing attacks.
2. Prevents Man-in-the-Middle attacks.
3. Validates IP-to-MAC bindings.
4. Uses DHCP Snooping database.
5. Improves Layer 2 security.
6. Protects default gateway information.
7. Provides logging and visibility.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Requires DHCP Snooping.
2. Static IP devices require additional configuration.
3. Extra switch processing is required.
4. Incorrect configuration can drop valid ARP packets.
5. More complex than basic Layer 2 security features.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even after validating ARP packets, attackers could still manually configure:

- Someone else's IP Address
- Someone else's MAC Address

and attempt to communicate on the network.

Administrators wanted a feature that checks whether a device is actually using the IP address assigned to it.

This led to:

IP Source Guard

which uses the DHCP Snooping Binding Table to verify source IP addresses before traffic is forwarded.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Prerequisite:
DHCP Snooping Must Be Enabled

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping vlan 10,20,30


Enable Dynamic ARP Inspection

Switch(config)# ip arp inspection vlan 10,20,30


Configure Trusted Uplink Port

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip arp inspection trust


User Ports Remain Untrusted
(Default)

Switch(config)# interface range fa0/1-24
Switch(config-if-range)# no ip arp inspection trust


(Optional) Configure ARP Rate Limit

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip arp inspection limit rate 20

Verification
============

show ip arp inspection

show ip arp inspection interfaces

show ip arp inspection statistics

show ip dhcp snooping binding

